  "result": "**Root cause:** `parseAmount` in `src/money.js` didn't accept prices with thousands separators. The logs show every failing checkout had a price like `\"1,299.00\"`, `\"2,450.50\"` or `\"1,005.99\"`. The regex didn't match those strings, so `match()` returned `null` and `m[1]` threw `TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading '1')`. That surfaced as a 500. The errors looked intermittent only because most orders contain no price of 1,000 or more.\n\n**Fix (`src/money.js`):**\n- `parseAmount` now accepts properly grouped thousands (`1,299.00`) and strips the commas before converting.\n- Input that isn't an amount now throws the documented `Error('Invalid amount: …')`. Before, it crashed with a `TypeError`. `src/server.js` still maps any thrown error to a 500, so malformed input still gets a 500. I didn't change that.\n\n**Tests (`test/money.test.js`):**\n- The three prices from the logs parse correctly.\n- Malformed amounts (`abc`, empty, `12,34`, `1,2345.00`, `1.234`) throw `Invalid amount`.\n- `checkout` totals an order that has a comma-formatted price.\n\n`npm test` passes with 7 of 7 tests. I didn't run the old code against the new tests to confirm they fail on it.",
